# PA-EP-002 — Enterprise Conceptual Information Model (ECIM)

**Project:** BTA V5.1 — HCGO Enterprise Persistence Architecture

**Status:** Approved

**Classification:** Persistence Architecture

---

# Purpose

Establish the authoritative Enterprise Conceptual Information Model (ECIM) for HCGO.

The Enterprise Conceptual Information Model (ECIM) is the authoritative conceptual representation of enterprise information and enterprise information relationships across HCGO.

It faithfully represents the enterprise semantic model established through Constitutional Governance, Enterprise Discovery, Enterprise Analysis, and Enterprise Persistence Architecture without introducing logical or physical implementation concerns.

The Enterprise Conceptual Information Model identifies enterprise concepts only.

It does not define logical data models, physical database design, PostgreSQL implementation, APIs, runtime execution, messaging, or technology.

---

# Architecture Objective

Establish the authoritative conceptual representation of enterprise information independently of technology, persistence implementation, runtime execution, or physical storage.

The Enterprise Conceptual Information Model serves as the authoritative semantic bridge between Enterprise Architecture and Persistence Design.

---

# Enterprise Conceptual Information Model

The Enterprise Conceptual Information Model shall identify:

- Enterprise Information Categories
- Enterprise Information Definitions
- Enterprise Information Authorities
- Enterprise Relationships
- Enterprise Constraints
- Enterprise Information Boundaries
- Enterprise Ownership Boundaries
- Enterprise Stewardship Boundaries

For every Enterprise Information Category identify:

- Enterprise Information Category
- Enterprise Definition
- Authoritative Owner
- Steward
- Enterprise Information Authorities
- Conceptual Relationships
- Enterprise Constraints
- Enterprise Information Boundaries
- Ownership Notes

---

# Enterprise Conceptual Information Model Does NOT Identify

The Enterprise Conceptual Information Model shall not identify:

- PostgreSQL tables
- Database entities
- Columns
- Foreign keys
- Indexes
- SQL
- APIs
- Runtime execution
- Service calls
- Logical implementation
- Physical persistence
- Technology implementation

Those responsibilities belong to subsequent architectural and implementation phases.

---

# Enterprise Conceptual Information Categories

The Enterprise Conceptual Information Model represents, at a minimum, the following Enterprise Information Categories:

- Enterprise Communication Normalization
- Enterprise Understanding
- Expert Identity
- Source Identity
- Participation Authorization
- Domain Definition
- Governance Metadata
- Enterprise Audit Evidence
- Platform Configuration
- Enterprise Capability Definition

Additional Enterprise Information Categories may be introduced through approved enterprise governance without redefining the conceptual model.

---

# Enterprise Conceptual Information Authorities

The Enterprise Conceptual Information Model conceptually represents enterprise authorities including:

- Authoritative Owner
- Creation Authority
- Modification Authority
- Archival Authority
- Retirement Authority
- Lifecycle Governance Authority

These authorities define conceptual enterprise responsibility.

They do not imply implementation responsibilities.

---

# Enterprise Conceptual Relationships

Conceptual relationships describe authoritative enterprise semantics only.

They identify conceptual relationships between Enterprise Information Categories without implying:

- Logical data structures
- Database relationships
- Foreign keys
- Runtime execution
- Orchestration
- APIs
- Persistence implementation
- Technology implementation

Logical Information Models shall faithfully represent these conceptual relationships.

They shall never redefine them.

---

# Enterprise Conceptual Constraints

The Enterprise Conceptual Information Model establishes enterprise constraints governing:

- Information ownership
- Information authority
- Stewardship
- Participation
- Governance
- Traceability
- Lifecycle
- Enterprise relationships

These constraints remain conceptual and technology independent.

---

# Enterprise Information Boundaries

Enterprise Information Boundaries define the conceptual limits within which enterprise information exists, is owned, is governed, and relates to other enterprise information.

Examples include:

- Domain Boundaries
- Capability Boundaries
- Information Boundaries
- Ownership Boundaries
- Stewardship Boundaries

These boundaries are enterprise semantic constructs.

They do not imply:

- Database schemas
- Tables
- Columns
- APIs
- Services
- Runtime execution
- Persistence implementation

Logical and physical persistence models shall faithfully represent these conceptual boundaries.

They shall never redefine them.

---

# Enterprise Principle

Every Enterprise Information Category represents an authoritative enterprise concept.

Conceptual relationships describe authoritative enterprise semantics only.

The Enterprise Conceptual Information Model establishes enterprise concepts independently of logical implementation, persistence structures, runtime execution, or technology.

---

# Architecture Outcome

The Enterprise Conceptual Information Model becomes the authoritative conceptual representation of enterprise information across HCGO.

It faithfully represents the enterprise semantic model established through:

- Constitutional Governance
- Enterprise Discovery
- Enterprise Analysis
- Enterprise Persistence Architecture

All subsequent Logical Information Models and Physical Persistence Models shall conform to the Enterprise Conceptual Information Model.

No logical or physical persistence artifact may redefine:

- Enterprise concepts
- Enterprise ownership
- Enterprise authorities
- Enterprise stewardship
- Enterprise relationships
- Enterprise constraints
- Enterprise information boundaries

established within the Enterprise Conceptual Information Model.

---

# Current Assessment

The Enterprise Conceptual Information Model establishes the authoritative semantic representation of enterprise information across HCGO.

It defines:

- Enterprise Information Categories
- Enterprise Information Definitions
- Enterprise Information Authorities
- Enterprise Relationships
- Enterprise Constraints
- Enterprise Information Boundaries
- Enterprise Ownership Boundaries
- Enterprise Stewardship Boundaries

Together these elements establish the complete conceptual semantic model that all subsequent Logical Information Models, Physical Persistence Models, PostgreSQL implementations, and future persistence technologies shall faithfully represent.

The Enterprise Conceptual Information Model remains the authoritative bridge between Enterprise Architecture and Persistence Design.

No subsequent architectural or implementation artifact may redefine the enterprise semantics established by the Enterprise Conceptual Information Model.